WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:00.767
More now on the

00:00:00.767 --> 00:00:02.168
health of actor Bruce Willis.

00:00:02.168 --> 00:00:02.902
His family

00:00:02.902 --> 00:00:03.670
revealing today

00:00:03.670 --> 00:00:04.604
that Willis is suffering

00:00:04.604 --> 00:00:05.772
from a disease called

00:00:05.772 --> 00:00:07.707
frontotemporal dementia.

00:00:07.707 --> 00:00:08.341
In a statement,

00:00:08.341 --> 00:00:09.109
they say that, quote,

00:00:09.109 --> 00:00:10.210
Since we announced versus

00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:13.246
diagnosis of aphasia in spring of 2022,

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:15.482
Bruce's condition has progressed

00:00:15.482 --> 00:00:16.483
and we now have a more

00:00:16.483 --> 00:00:17.851
specific diagnosis.

00:00:17.851 --> 00:00:20.587
Frontotemporal dementia known as FTD.

00:00:20.887 --> 00:00:22.322
Unfortunately, challenges

00:00:22.322 --> 00:00:23.923
with communication are just one

00:00:23.923 --> 00:00:26.259
symptom of the disease that Bruce faces.

00:00:26.259 --> 00:00:27.427
While this is painful,

00:00:27.427 --> 00:00:29.429
it is a relief to finally have

00:00:29.429 --> 00:00:31.264
a clear diagnosis.

00:00:31.264 --> 00:00:33.066
I'm joined by CNN medical analyst Dr.

00:00:33.066 --> 00:00:33.767
Jonathan Reiner.

00:00:33.767 --> 00:00:35.969
Dr. Reiner, great to see you tonight.

00:00:36.002 --> 00:00:38.171
So what is frontotemporal dementia

00:00:38.171 --> 00:00:40.707
and how common is it

00:00:41.875 --> 00:00:43.643
So in frontotemporal

00:00:43.643 --> 00:00:47.680
dementia is sort of an umbrella term that

00:00:48.715 --> 00:00:51.251
describes a series of conditions

00:00:51.251 --> 00:00:55.088
that ultimately result in the loss

00:00:55.088 --> 00:00:56.523
of neural tissue

00:00:56.523 --> 00:00:57.624
in the frontal

00:00:57.624 --> 00:00:59.559
and temporal lobes of the brain.

00:00:59.559 --> 00:01:00.693
The frontal lobes

00:01:00.693 --> 00:01:03.063
sort of behind your forehead,

00:01:03.329 --> 00:01:04.898
the temporal lobes

00:01:04.898 --> 00:01:06.766
sort of behind your ears.

00:01:06.766 --> 00:01:08.101
Those are the parts of the brain

00:01:08.101 --> 00:01:11.071
that are involved with helping you

00:01:12.238 --> 00:01:14.641
form certain behaviors,

00:01:14.641 --> 00:01:17.744
process, hearing, form memories.

00:01:18.211 --> 00:01:20.680
And ultimately it results in

00:01:21.648 --> 00:01:23.683
clinical conditions that sometimes

00:01:23.683 --> 00:01:25.718
radically change the behavior

00:01:25.718 --> 00:01:27.253
of a patient

00:01:27.253 --> 00:01:29.956
or result in a difficulty communicate

00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:33.093
And I think last year, when

00:01:33.093 --> 00:01:33.326
Mr.

00:01:33.326 --> 00:01:34.561
Wilson's family reported

00:01:34.561 --> 00:01:36.563
that he was suffering from aphasia,

00:01:36.563 --> 00:01:39.599
that was the first clue that

00:01:39.599 --> 00:01:41.634
he had frontotemporal dementia,

00:01:41.634 --> 00:01:43.036
which often involves

00:01:43.036 --> 00:01:44.571
some form of aphasia.

00:01:44.571 --> 00:01:46.439
What causes this

00:01:47.774 --> 00:01:49.409
It's a

00:01:49.876 --> 00:01:51.911
disease with multiple causes.

00:01:52.645 --> 00:01:53.413
There is

00:01:53.413 --> 00:01:57.250
sometimes a genetic component of some.

00:01:57.484 --> 00:01:59.719
This can run in families.

00:02:00.086 --> 00:02:02.188
It's a relatively rare disease

00:02:02.188 --> 00:02:04.791
seen in about 50,000 Americans.

00:02:05.225 --> 00:02:05.892
But it's it's

00:02:05.892 --> 00:02:08.962
probably the most common form of dementia

00:02:08.962 --> 00:02:10.463
in young people,

00:02:10.463 --> 00:02:11.464
particularly in people

00:02:11.464 --> 00:02:14.267
between the ages of 45

00:02:14.501 --> 00:02:16.336
and 65 years of age.

00:02:16.336 --> 00:02:17.270
But overall,

00:02:17.270 --> 00:02:18.271
this type of dementia

00:02:18.271 --> 00:02:19.706
probably doesn't account

00:02:19.706 --> 00:02:20.106
for more

00:02:20.106 --> 00:02:20.473
than about

00:02:20.473 --> 00:02:23.877
20% of the different forms of dementia

00:02:24.511 --> 00:02:26.713
that occur in people as they age.

00:02:27.447 --> 00:02:28.615
And what are the symptoms.

00:02:28.615 --> 00:02:30.183
I mean we've been told by his family

00:02:30.183 --> 00:02:31.951
that he's having communication issues

00:02:31.951 --> 00:02:33.453
and it's been written about that.

00:02:33.453 --> 00:02:35.155
He's been in movies recently

00:02:35.155 --> 00:02:35.722
but he was,

00:02:35.722 --> 00:02:37.190
you know, had very small parts

00:02:37.190 --> 00:02:38.992
and was sort of fed lines.

00:02:38.992 --> 00:02:41.094
Had to be Fed lines. What other symptoms

00:02:43.396 --> 00:02:43.763
Right.

00:02:43.763 --> 00:02:44.397
So that may be

00:02:44.397 --> 00:02:44.631
that he

00:02:44.631 --> 00:02:47.400
was having difficulty with memory.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:49.235
So as I said earlier,

00:02:49.235 --> 00:02:50.670
some people have difficulty

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:52.839
actually in completing sentences

00:02:52.839 --> 00:02:55.975
or forming coherent thoughts.

00:02:56.943 --> 00:02:59.979
Some patients will develop

00:02:59.979 --> 00:03:01.748
outrageous behaviors,

00:03:01.748 --> 00:03:03.850
a sort of lack of any inhibition,

00:03:04.651 --> 00:03:07.453
increase in sexual activity,

00:03:07.453 --> 00:03:09.622
bizarre outbursts,

00:03:09.622 --> 00:03:11.157
particularly when the disease

00:03:11.157 --> 00:03:13.293
involves the frontal lobes.

00:03:13.293 --> 00:03:15.962
So the behavior of patients

00:03:15.962 --> 00:03:18.364
can be very disturbing to families

00:03:18.364 --> 00:03:20.700
that people can live with this disease.

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:24.404
For you know, for years, which is

00:03:25.872 --> 00:03:27.540
sometimes a curse.

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:28.341
What a challenge.

00:03:28.341 --> 00:03:29.642
What a challenge for the family,

00:03:29.642 --> 00:03:31.211
everything you've just described.

00:03:31.211 --> 00:03:35.114
Are there treatments for this disease?

00:03:35.448 --> 00:03:38.918
There's no cure for the disease, but

00:03:39.219 --> 00:03:40.386
patients can be treated

00:03:40.386 --> 00:03:43.723
with anxiolytic drugs to treat anxiety

00:03:43.957 --> 00:03:47.360
to help calm some of the outbursts.

00:03:48.061 --> 00:03:50.363
Sometimes anti-doping lessons

00:03:50.363 --> 00:03:52.532
can help, but there is really no

00:03:54.133 --> 00:03:55.201
cure for this disease.

00:03:55.201 --> 00:03:56.803
It's a progressive disease

00:03:56.803 --> 00:03:58.938
that ultimately will result in death

00:03:58.938 --> 00:04:01.774
But it can take many years.

00:04:01.774 --> 00:04:02.709
It really is a curse,

00:04:02.709 --> 00:04:04.777
as you describe it, because you

00:04:04.777 --> 00:04:06.546
it sounds like the body.

00:04:06.546 --> 00:04:07.680
And he's a strong man.

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:10.016
I mean, he's a strong fit man.

00:04:10.250 --> 00:04:11.985
And so for his brain

00:04:11.985 --> 00:04:13.386
to be betraying him in

00:04:13.386 --> 00:04:15.421
this way will be a huge challenge

00:04:17.257 --> 00:04:19.325
right now.

00:04:19.325 --> 00:04:19.892
Dr. Reiner.

00:04:19.892 --> 00:04:21.928
And this can and this can go on.

00:04:21.928 --> 00:04:22.762
I'm sorry.

00:04:22.762 --> 00:04:22.996
Go.

00:04:22.996 --> 00:04:24.564
You can finish your son's

00:04:25.832 --> 00:04:26.799
Now, I was going to say, you know,

00:04:26.799 --> 00:04:29.102
this can go on for many years,

00:04:29.102 --> 00:04:30.970
and it becomes

00:04:30.970 --> 00:04:32.505
it can become a tremendous burden

00:04:32.505 --> 00:04:33.172
for families

00:04:33.172 --> 00:04:33.940
to care

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:34.440
for people

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:36.709
affected with these conditions.

00:04:36.709 --> 00:04:38.278
Yeah, I can imagine.

00:04:38.278 --> 00:04:39.412
Dr. Reiner, it's sad.

00:04:39.412 --> 00:04:40.313
Thank you very much

00:04:40.313 --> 00:04:42.148
for all the information.

00:04:42.148 --> 00:04:43.483
Nice to have you with us tonight.

00:04:45.318 --> 00:04:45.918
Thanks, Alisyn.

